// PharosGRC — Prisma schema.
//
// Phase 2 data layer (T026, T027, T028, T033, T037) plus the supporting
// Core models their foreign keys require (User, LocalCredential,
// ExternalIdentity, Session — nominally T057, implemented here because
// audit_event, RolePermission/UserRole and OrgScopeGrant cannot reference a
// User table that does not exist).
//
// Spec: specs/001-grc-platform-foundation/data-model.md, sections 1-5.
// ADRs: specs/001-grc-platform-foundation/research.md — ADR-001 (identity),
// ADR-002 (sessions), ADR-003 (RBAC), ADR-004 (materialized path),
// ADR-005 (append-only audit), ADR-008 (explicit relational modeling).
//
// ## Naming convention
//
// data-model.md names one table explicitly in snake_case (`audit_event`) and
// otherwise only gives PascalCase section headers for the others. No naming
// convention document existed before this schema, so this file establishes
// one and applies it uniformly: Prisma models are PascalCase (idiomatic
// Prisma/TypeScript), mapped via `@@map`/`@map` to snake_case tables and
// columns (idiomatic PostgreSQL, and consistent with the one table name the
// spec gives literally). A future agent adding models should follow the
// same mapping.
//
// ## Field lists: literal-vs-blanket tension — RESOLVED by D-7
//
// data-model.md's "Shared conventions" preamble states every table carries
// `createdAt`/`updatedAt`, while every per-table field list in sections 1-4
// except `User` originally omitted them. This was deliberately implemented
// literally at first (see git history) and flagged as a spec tension. The
// coordinator resolved it as **D-7** (docs/local-environment.md):
// `createdAt`/`updatedAt` were added to the mutable entity tables that
// lacked them — `LocalCredential`, `ExternalIdentity`, `Permission`, `Role`,
// `OrganizationalUnit` — via an additive, forward-only migration
// (`20260905130000_createdat_updatedat_reconciliation`) backfilling
// existing rows with `now()`. Deliberately EXCLUDED, per the same decision:
// join tables (`RolePermission`, `UserRole`, `OrgScopeGrant` — their
// `grantedAt` already carries the semantic timestamp), `Session`
// (`createdAt` + `lastActivityAt` are the meaningful instants), and
// `audit_event` (append-only; `occurredAt` is the timestamp, and a mutable
// `updatedAt` on an immutable table would be incoherent). See D-7 for the
// full rule and rationale.
//
// ## audit_event / partitioning drift (read before touching this table)
//
// `audit_event` is converted to a monthly `PARTITION BY RANGE (occurredAt)`
// table by a hand-written SQL migration (T027) run immediately after the
// baseline migration this schema produces, and made append-only by another
// hand-written SQL migration (T028). Prisma cannot express `PARTITION BY`,
// partition child tables, the default partition, the append-only trigger,
// or per-role GRANT/REVOKE — none of that can appear in this file. What
// CAN be kept truthful here — and is — is the composite primary key
// `(id, occurredAt)`, because PostgreSQL requires a partitioned table's
// primary key to include the partition key. Do not "fix" this back to a
// single-column `id` primary key: that would fight the real schema.
//
// Consequence for future migrations: `prisma migrate dev` computes its
// diff by replaying every migration (including the T027/T028 raw SQL)
// against a shadow database and comparing the result to this file. The
// shadow database will have a partitioned `audit_event` with child
// partitions, a trigger and non-default privileges; this file describes
// only the parent table's columns and composite key. That is an intended,
// permanent, irreducible difference — `prisma migrate dev` may propose a
// migration touching `audit_event` (e.g. it may believe indexes changed,
// since Prisma is unaware partitions exist). NEVER accept an
// auto-generated migration that drops/recreates `audit_event`. Any future
// change to `audit_event` must be a hand-written, reviewed SQL migration,
// exactly like T027/T028. Prefer `prisma migrate deploy` (never
// `migrate dev`) against real databases for this project; if `migrate dev`
// must be used to author a new migration for an unrelated table, review
// the generated SQL and delete any `audit_event` statements it proposes
// before applying.

generator client {
  provider        = "prisma-client-js"
  previewFeatures = ["postgresqlExtensions"]
}

datasource db {
  provider   = "postgresql"
  url        = env("DATABASE_URL")
  directUrl  = env("DATABASE_MIGRATION_URL")
  extensions = [citext, pg_trgm]
}

